+<p><strong>Proof of √2 irrational by infinite descent:</strong></p>
+<p>Assume √2 = a/b in lowest terms (gcd(a,b)=1). Then:</p>
+<pre><code>a² = 2b²  →  a even → a = 2k
+4k² = 2b² → b² = 2k² → b even → b = 2m
+</code></pre>
+<p>This produces a smaller pair (k,m) representing √2, where k &lt; a and m &lt; b. Repeating gives an infinite strictly decreasing sequence of positive integers, contradicting the Well-Ordering Principle. Therefore no such a,b exist → √2 is irrational.</p>
+<p><strong>Corollary — √n irrational for non-perfect-square n:</strong></p>
+<p>If n is not a perfect square, its prime factorization contains a prime p with odd exponent e. From a² = n·b², LHS has even exponent of p (2·α), RHS has odd exponent (e + 2·β). Contradiction via uniqueness of prime factorization.</p>
